.TH HASH-SHELL 1 "November 2026" "hash-shell 53" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hash-shell \- modern, POSIX-compliant command line interpreter for Linux, macOS, and BSD
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hash-shell
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ script
.RI [ arguments... ]]
.br
.B hash-shell
.B \-c
.I command_string
.RI [ arguments... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hash-shell
is a comprehensive C-based command line shell interpreter providing a modern,
secure, and POSIX-compliant alternative to traditional shells like bash and zsh.
It features git-aware prompts, extensive tab completion, job control, and full
scripting support with control structures.
.PP
When invoked without arguments,
.B hash-shell
runs interactively, reading commands from standard input.
When given a script file, it executes the commands in that file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " command_string"
Execute commands from
.I command_string
instead of reading from standard input or a file. The arguments after it
become $0, $1 and so on.
.TP
.B \-i
Run interactively even when standard input is not a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Run as a login shell: read /etc/profile and ~/.hash_profile (or ~/.profile),
and ~/.hash_logout on exit.
.TP
.B \-s
Read commands from standard input. Useful when piping commands to the shell.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-b ", " \-C ", " \-e ", " \-f ", " \-m ", " \-n ", " \-u ", " \-x
Set the shell option of that letter, as
.B set
does (see SHELL BUILTIN COMMANDS).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage message and exit.
.SH INVOCATION
.TP
.B Interactive shell
When started without a script argument and with a terminal connected,
.B hash-shell
reads and executes commands interactively. The configuration file
.I ~/.hashrc
is read and executed on startup.
.TP
.B Non-interactive shell
When reading commands from a script file,
.B hash-shell
executes them without loading
.IR ~/.hashrc .
.SH SHELL GRAMMAR
.SS Simple Commands
A simple command is a sequence of optional variable assignments followed by
blank-separated words and redirections.
.SS Pipelines
A pipeline is a sequence of one or more commands separated by
.BR | .
The standard output of each command except the last is connected to the
standard input of the next command.
.SS Lists
A list is a sequence of one or more pipelines separated by one of the
operators
.BR ; ,
.BR && ,
or
.BR || ,
and optionally terminated by
.BR ; .
.TP
.BR "command1 ; command2"
Execute command2 after command1, regardless of command1's exit status.
.TP
.BR "command1 && command2"
Execute command2 only if command1 succeeds (exit status 0).
.TP
.BR "command1 || command2"
Execute command2 only if command1 fails (non-zero exit status).
.SH CONTROL STRUCTURES
.SS If/Then/Else
.nf
.B if
.I command
.B ; then
    .I commands
.B [elif
.I command
.B ; then
    .I commands
.B ] ...
.B [else
    .I commands
.B ]
.B fi
.fi
.SS For Loops
.nf
.B for
.I name
.B in
.I words
.B ; do
    .I commands
.B done
.fi
.SS While Loops
.nf
.B while
.I command
.B ; do
    .I commands
.B done
.fi
.SS Until Loops
.nf
.B until
.I command
.B ; do
    .I commands
.B done
.fi
.SS Case Statement
.nf
.B case
.I word
.B in
    .I pattern
.B )
        .I commands
.B ;;
    ...
.B esac
.fi
.SH QUOTING
Quoting is used to remove the special meaning of certain characters.
.TP
.B Single quotes
Preserve literal value of all characters within the quotes.
.TP
.B Double quotes
Preserve literal value of all characters except
.BR $ ,
.BR ` ,
and
.BR \e .
Variable expansion occurs within double quotes.
.TP
.B Backslash
A non-quoted backslash preserves the literal value of the next character.
.TP
.B Dollar-single quotes
.BI $' string '
is like single quotes, but backslash escapes are decoded first: \en, \et,
\e\e, \e', \exHH, \ennn, \euHHHH and the rest, as in bash and ksh.
.SH PARAMETERS
.SS Positional Parameters
.TP
.B $0
The name of the shell or script.
.TP
.B $1 \- $9
Positional parameters passed to the script or function.
.TP
.B $#
The number of positional parameters.
.TP
.B $@
All positional parameters as separate words.
.TP
.B $*
All positional parameters as a single word.
.TP
.B $?
Exit status of the most recently executed foreground pipeline.
.TP
.B $$
Process ID of the shell.
.TP
.B $!
Process ID of the most recent background command.
.TP
.B $\-
The option letters in effect (see
.BR set ).
.TP
.B $LINENO
Line number of the current command within the script, function or command string.
.SS Variable Expansion
.TP
.B $VAR
Expands to the value of variable VAR.
.TP
.B ${VAR}
Expands to the value of variable VAR. Useful for concatenation.
.TP
.BR ${VAR\-word} ", " ${VAR:\-word} ", " ${VAR=word} ", " ${VAR:=word} ", " ${VAR+word} ", " ${VAR:+word} ", " ${VAR?word} ", " ${VAR:?word}
Use a default, assign a default, use an alternative, or fail with a message
when VAR is unset (with the colon, also when it is empty).
.TP
.B ${#VAR}
The length of the value.
.TP
.BR ${VAR#pattern} ", " ${VAR##pattern} ", " ${VAR%pattern} ", " ${VAR%%pattern}
The value with the shortest or longest match of pattern removed from the
start or from the end.
.SH REDIRECTION
.TP
.BI < " file"
Redirect standard input from
.IR file .
.TP
.BI > " file"
Redirect standard output to
.IR file ,
creating or truncating it.
.TP
.BI >> " file"
Append standard output to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI 2> " file"
Redirect standard error to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI 2>> " file"
Append standard error to
.IR file .
.TP
.B 2>&1
Redirect standard error to standard output.
.TP
.BI &> " file"
Redirect both standard output and standard error to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI >| " file"
Redirect standard output to
.IR file ,
overwriting it even under
.BR "set \-C" .
.TP
.BI n> " file" ", " n< " file"
Redirect file descriptor n.
.TP
.BI n>& m ", " n<& m
Make descriptor n a copy of descriptor m.
.TP
.BI n>& \-
Close descriptor n.
.TP
.BI << " word"
Here-document: the following lines, up to a line holding only word, become
the standard input. Quoting word keeps the body literal; otherwise parameters
and command substitutions expand in it.
.BI <<\- " word"
strips leading tabs from the body.
.SH JOB CONTROL
.B hash-shell
supports job control, allowing you to run commands in the background
and manage multiple processes.
.TP
.BI "command " &
Run
.I command
in the background.
.TP
.B jobs
List all background jobs with their status.
.TP
.BI fg " [%job]"
Bring a background job to the foreground. Without an argument, brings the
current job.
.TP
.BI bg " [%job]"
Resume a stopped job in the background.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z
Suspend the current foreground job.
.PP
Job specifications:
.RS
.TP
.B %n
Job number n.
.TP
.B %%
Current job.
.TP
.B %+
Current job (same as %%).
.TP
.B %-
Previous job.
.RE
.SH TEST COMMAND
The
.B test
command (or
.BR [ " ]" )
evaluates conditional expressions, returning 0 for true and 1 for false.
.SS File Tests
.TP
.BI \-e " file"
True if file exists.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
True if file exists and is a regular file.
.TP
.BI \-d " file"
True if file exists and is a directory.
.TP
.BI \-r " file"
True if file exists and is readable.
.TP
.BI \-w " file"
True if file exists and is writable.
.TP
.BI \-x " file"
True if file exists and is executable.
.TP
.BI \-s " file"
True if file exists and has size greater than zero.
.TP
.BI \-L " file"
True if file is a symbolic link.
.SS String Tests
.TP
.BI \-z " string"
True if string is empty.
.TP
.BI \-n " string"
True if string is not empty.
.TP
.IB s1 " = " s2
True if strings are equal.
.TP
.IB s1 " != " s2
True if strings are not equal.
.SS Numeric Tests
.TP
.IB n1 " \-eq " n2
True if n1 equals n2.
.TP
.IB n1 " \-ne " n2
True if n1 does not equal n2.
.TP
.IB n1 " \-lt " n2
True if n1 is less than n2.
.TP
.IB n1 " \-le " n2
True if n1 is less than or equal to n2.
.TP
.IB n1 " \-gt " n2
True if n1 is greater than n2.
.TP
.IB n1 " \-ge " n2
True if n1 is greater than or equal to n2.
.SS Logical Operators
.TP
.BI ! " expr"
Logical NOT.
.TP
.IB expr1 " \-a " expr2
Logical AND.
.TP
.IB expr1 " \-o " expr2
Logical OR.
.SS Extended Test [[ ]]
.B hash-shell
also supports the extended test
.B [[ ]]
syntax with additional features:
.TP
Pattern matching with
.B ==
and
.B !=
.TP
Regular expression matching with
.B =~
.TP
String comparison with
.B <
and
.B >
.TP
Logical operators
.B &&
and
.B ||
inside the brackets
.TP
.BI \-v " VAR"
True if variable VAR is set
.SH COMMAND HISTORY
.B hash-shell
maintains a history of commands that can be navigated and recalled.
.TP
.B Up/Down Arrow
Navigate through command history.
.TP
.B !!
Execute the previous command.
.TP
.BI ! n
Execute command number n from history.
.TP
.BI ! prefix
Execute the most recent command starting with prefix.
.TP
.BI !\- n
Execute the command n entries back.
.PP
A command typed over several lines (a loop, an if, a function) is one history
entry and comes back whole. The history is saved to ~/.hash_history as it is
entered, in bash's extended format (a line with the time before each entry);
.B history
shows it and
.B history \-c
clears it (see SHELL BUILTIN COMMANDS). HISTSIZE, HISTFILESIZE and HISTCONTROL
tune it (see ENVIRONMENT).
.SH SHELL BUILTIN COMMANDS
.\" docs-parity: generated from the builtin table in src/builtins.c
.\" by scripts/docs-parity.py; `help NAME` prints the same text. Edit the
.\" table, then run: make docs
Every builtin takes \-\- as the end of its options. In the shell,
.B help
lists these entries and
.B help NAME
prints one; the book keeps the same text at
https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html .
.TP
\fB\&.\fR file
Read and run file in the current shell (POSIX spelling of source). `return`
ends the file; `exit` ends the shell.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fB:\fR [arg...]
Do nothing; the arguments are expanded and dropped. Status 0.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fB[\fR expr ]
The test command written with brackets; the closing ] is required. See
`help test` for the operators.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/scripting/test\-command.html
.TP
\fB[[\fR expr ]]
Extended test: the operators of test plus pattern matching with == and !=,
regular expressions with =~, and && and || inside the brackets. Words are
not split or glob\-expanded.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/scripting/test\-command.html
.TP
\fBalias\fR [name[=value]...]
Define aliases, or print them: `alias` lists every alias, `alias name` prints
one. An alias is expanded when its name is the first word of a command.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/getting\-started/hashrc.html
.TP
\fBbg\fR [%job]
Continue a stopped job in the background.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/operators/background.html
.TP
\fBbreak\fR [n]
Leave the innermost n enclosing loops (one by default).
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBcd\fR [\-L|\-P] [dir]
Change the working directory to dir, or to $HOME without one. \-L, the default,
keeps the logical path, so `cd ..` under a symlinked directory goes to the
parent of the link; \-P follows symlinks and sets PWD to the physical path.
`cd \-` goes back to the previous directory and prints it. PWD and OLDPWD are
updated.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBcommand\fR name [arg...]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBcommand\fR \-v | \-V name...
.PD
Run name without looking up functions, so a function can call the command
it shadows; a failing special builtin does not end the shell, but break,
continue, return and exit keep their effect. \-v prints how name would be
resolved, \-V describes it.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBcomplete\fR [\-abcdfuv] [\-o default|dirnames] [\-W words] [\-F function] name...
.PD 0
.TP
\fBcomplete\fR \-p [name...]
.TP
\fBcomplete\fR \-r [name...]
.PD
Record how a command's arguments are tab\-completed. \-W gives the candidate
words, \-F a function whose output lines are candidates ($1 command, $2 word,
$3 previous word), \-a \-b \-c \-d \-f \-u \-v add aliases, builtins, commands,
directories, files, users or variables, \-o default falls back to files.
\-p prints the definitions in a form that can be typed back, \-r removes them.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/shell\-features/tab\-completion.html
.TP
\fBcontinue\fR [n]
Resume the next iteration of the innermost n enclosing loops.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBecho\fR [\-neE] [arg...]
Print the arguments separated by spaces, then a newline. \-n leaves out the
newline, \-e interprets backslash escapes (\en \et \e\e ...), \-E does not.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBeval\fR [arg...]
Join the arguments with spaces and run the result as a command in the
current shell.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBexec\fR [command [arg...]]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBexec\fR redirection...
.PD
Replace the shell with command. With only redirections, apply them to the
shell itself for the commands that follow (`exec 3< file`, `exec >log`).
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/operators/redirection.html
.TP
\fBexit\fR [n]
Leave the shell with status n, or with the status of the last command. The
EXIT trap runs first; a login shell also runs ~/.hash_logout.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBexport\fR [\-p] [name[=value]...]
Mark variables for export to the environment of commands, assigning a value
when one is given. \-p, or no arguments, prints the exported variables.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBfalse\fR
Do nothing, with status 1.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBfg\fR [%job]
Bring a job to the foreground and wait for it. Without an argument, the
current job.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/operators/background.html
.TP
\fBgetopts\fR optstring name [arg...]
Parse options from the positional parameters (or arg...) one call at a time,
placing the letter in name, its argument in OPTARG and the next index in
OPTIND. A colon after a letter in optstring means it takes an argument; a
leading colon selects silent error reporting.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBhash\fR [\-r] [name...]
Remember where commands live. With names, look them up now; \-r forgets
everything remembered; alone, print the table.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBhelp\fR [\-s] [pattern...]
Describe the builtins and the shell's keywords. Alone, list every topic with
its synopsis; with names or glob patterns, print the synopsis and a short
description of each; \-s prints the synopsis only. The book has the same
text at https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBhistory\fR [\-c | \-w | \-r]
Print the command history with its numbers. \-c clears it, \-w writes it to
the history file, \-r reads the file back. HISTFILE, HISTSIZE, HISTFILESIZE
and HISTCONTROL apply; `!!`, `!n`, `!\-n` and `!prefix` expand entries.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/shell\-features/history.html
.TP
\fBjobs\fR [\-l | \-p]
List the background jobs of this shell. \-l adds the process IDs, \-p prints
only them.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/operators/background.html
.TP
\fBkill\fR [\-s signal | \-signal] pid | %job ...
.PD 0
.TP
\fBkill\fR \-l
.PD
Send a signal (TERM by default) to processes or jobs, by number or name.
\-l lists the signal names.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/operators/background.html
.TP
\fBlocal\fR [name[=value]...]
Declare variables local to the current function; the previous values come
back when it returns. Outside a function it is an error.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/scripting/index.html#functions
.TP
\fBlogout\fR
Leave a login shell, running ~/.hash_logout first. In a shell that is not a
login shell it fails; use exit.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBpwd\fR [\-L|\-P]
Print the working directory. \-L (the default) prints $PWD, the path
cd built, when it still names the current directory; \-P prints the
physical path with every symbolic link resolved.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBread\fR [\-r] name...
Read one line from standard input and split it into the variables; the last
one takes the rest. Backslash continues a line unless \-r is given. The
status is 1 at end of input.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBreadonly\fR [\-p] [name[=value]...]
Mark variables read\-only, assigning a value when one is given; later
assignments and unset fail. \-p, or no arguments, prints them.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBreturn\fR [n]
Leave the current function or sourced file with status n, or with the status
of the last command.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/scripting/index.html#functions
.TP
\fBset\fR [\-abCefhmnuvx] [\-o option] [+...] [\-\-] [arg...]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBset\fR name=value
.PD
Set shell options with \-, clear them with +: \-a allexport, \-b notify,
\-C noclobber, \-e errexit, \-f noglob, \-h, \-m monitor, \-n noexec, \-u nounset,
\-v verbose, \-x xtrace; \-o takes a name, also emacs, vi, nolog and
nonlexicalctrl. `set \-o` lists the options, `set +o` prints them as
commands, $\- holds the letters in effect. Arguments after the options (or
after \-\-) become the positional parameters.
hash's own settings use name=value: prompt=NAME picks a prompt preset
(classic, plain, minimal, two\-line, powerline; prompt=list shows them),
PS1=... builds one, and colors=on|off, welcome=on|off, color.<element>=<spec>,
syntax_highlight=on|off, autosuggest=on|off, danger_highlight=on|off. They
work at the prompt, in scripts and in ~/.hashrc.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/index.html#shell\-options
.TP
\fBshift\fR [n]
Drop the first n positional parameters (one by default). A count past $# is
an error.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBsource\fR file
Read and run file in the current shell, like `.`. Functions, variables and
aliases it defines stay. A file name without a slash is looked up in PATH.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBtest\fR expr
Evaluate a conditional expression: file tests (\-e \-f \-d \-r \-w \-x \-s \-L and
more), string tests (\-z \-n = !=), numbers (\-eq \-ne \-lt \-le \-gt \-ge), and !
\-a \-o with parentheses. The status is 0 when expr is true. `[ expr ]` is the
same command with a closing bracket.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/scripting/test\-command.html
.TP
\fBtimes\fR
Print the user and system times used by the shell and by its children.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBtrap\fR [action] [signal...]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBtrap\fR \-l | \-p
.PD
Run action when a signal arrives, or at EXIT. An empty action ignores the
signal, `\-` restores the default. \-l lists the signal names, \-p prints the
traps in effect. The EXIT trap sees the shell's status as $?.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBtrue\fR
Do nothing, successfully.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBtype\fR [\-afptP] name...
Say how each name would be interpreted: an alias, a shell keyword, a
function, a builtin, or a file found in PATH, in that order. \-t prints
one word (alias, keyword, function, builtin, file); \-p prints the file
that would run and nothing for anything else; \-P searches PATH even when
an alias, function or builtin would run; \-a prints every match; \-f skips
functions. Status 1 when a name is not found.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBulimit\fR [\-HS] [\-a | \-cdfnstuv] [limit]
Show or set a resource limit of the shell and the commands it runs.
\-c core file (512\-byte blocks), \-d data (KB), \-f file size (blocks),
\-n open files, \-s stack (KB), \-t CPU seconds, \-u processes, \-v
virtual memory (KB); \-f when no resource is named. \-a shows them all.
\-H acts on the hard limit, \-S on the soft one (both when setting,
the soft one when showing). The limit is a number or unlimited.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBumask\fR [\-S] [mode]
Show or set the file creation mask. Without a mode, print it in octal,
or with \-S as u=rwx,g=rx,o=rx. A mode is octal (022) or symbolic
(u+r, g\-w,o=, a=rx) as for chmod.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBunalias\fR [\-a] name...
Remove the named aliases, or every alias with \-a. A name that is not an
alias is reported and the status is 1; the other names are still removed.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/getting\-started/hashrc.html
.TP
\fBunset\fR [\-f | \-v] name...
Remove variables (\-v, the default) or functions (\-f). Readonly variables
cannot be unset.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBupdate\fR
Check for a newer release of hash and install it when one is available.
HASH_DISABLE_UPDATE_CHECK=1 turns the startup check off.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/reference/builtins.html
.TP
\fBwait\fR [pid | %job ...]
Wait for the given background jobs, or for all of them, and return the
status of the last one waited for.
.br
More: https://hash\-shell.org/operators/background.html
.\" docs-parity: end of the generated entries
.SH PROMPT CUSTOMIZATION
.B set prompt=NAME
picks one of the built-in prompts: classic (the default), plain
(user@host:path$), minimal, two\-line and powerline;
.B set prompt=list
shows them all. PS1 builds your own, with these escape sequences:
.TP
.B \ew
Full current working directory path (~ for home).
.TP
.B \eW
Current directory name only.
.TP
.B \eu
Username.
.TP
.B \eh
Hostname, up to the first dot;
.B \eH
is the full host name.
.TP
.B \eg
Git branch with status indicator. Shows branch name in cyan, with green
"git:" if clean or yellow "git:" if there are uncommitted changes.
.B \eb
is the branch name alone, and nothing outside a repository.
.TP
.B \ee
Exit code color indicator. Text between \ee...\ee is colored blue for
success (exit code 0) or red for failure (non-zero exit code).
.B \e?
is the last exit status when it was not 0, otherwise nothing.
.TP
.B \e$
Shows $ for regular users, # for root.
.TP
.BR \et ", " \eT ", " \e@ ", " \ed
The time (24\-hour, 12\-hour, 12\-hour with am/pm) and the date.
.TP
.BR \ej ", " \e!
The number of background jobs, and the history number of the next command.
.TP
.BR \es ", " \ev
The shell name and version.
.TP
.B \en
Newline.
.TP
.B \e\e
Literal backslash.
.TP
.BR \e[ ", " \e]
Ignored: bash wraps colour codes in them, hash measures the codes itself.
.PP
PS2 is the continuation prompt, shown while a command is completed over
several lines.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
set PS1='\eW\eg \ee#>\ee '      # Default: directory git:(branch) #>
set PS1='\eu@\eh:\ew\e$ '      # Classic: user@host:/path $
set PS1='\ew\eg\en\ee#>\ee '   # Two-line with git info
.fi
.RE
.SH TAB COMPLETION
.B hash-shell
completes what the command expects:
.TP
.B Commands
The first word completes from aliases, functions, builtins, keywords and the
executables in PATH.
.TP
.B Files and directories
Arguments complete to file names, with a trailing / for a directory;
.BR cd ", " pushd " and " rmdir
offer directories only. ~ and ~user expand.
.TP
.B Variables and users
$NA and ${NA complete variable names, ~na user names.
.TP
.B Commands that know their arguments
.B unalias
completes alias names;
.BR unset ", " export ", " readonly " and " local
variable names;
.BR command ", " exec ", " type ", " sudo ", " nohup ", " time ", " env ", " xargs
and friends a command name first, then files;
.B git
its subcommands, then branches, tags, remotes or files;
.B make
the targets of the Makefile in the current directory;
.B man
page names.
.TP
.B complete
records completions for other commands:
.B complete \-W words name
offers the words,
.B complete \-F function name
runs the function and takes its output lines (see SHELL BUILTIN COMMANDS).
.PP
Press Tab once to complete or show matches. Press Tab again to cycle
through multiple matches. Options such as \-\-long are not completed.
.SH LINE EDITING
Interactive line editing is supported with the following key bindings:
.TP
.B Left/Right Arrow
Move cursor within the line.
.TP
.B Home, Ctrl+A
Move to beginning of line.
.TP
.B End, Ctrl+E
Move to end of line.
.TP
.B Backspace, Ctrl+H
Delete character before cursor.
.TP
.B Delete, Ctrl+D
Delete character at cursor.
.TP
.B Ctrl+U
Delete from cursor to beginning of line.
.TP
.B Ctrl+K
Delete from cursor to end of line.
.TP
.B Ctrl+W
Delete word before cursor.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Cancel current line.
.TP
.B Ctrl+L
Clear screen.
.TP
.B Enter, Ctrl+J
Run the line.
.PP
Text typed while a command runs is kept and appears at the next prompt. With
.B set \-o vi
the editor switches to vi's modes: Esc enters command mode with the usual
motions (h l 0 ^ $ w b e), operators (d c y with a motion, dd cc yy D C, x X,
p P, r, ~, u), k and j for history and / for an incremental history search;
i a I A return to insert mode.
.B set \-o emacs
is the default.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.B hash-shell
supports shell functions:
.PP
.nf
.I name
.B () {
    .I commands
.B }
.fi
.PP
Functions are called like commands and can access arguments via $1, $2, etc.
Use
.B return
to exit a function with a specific exit status, and
.B local
for variables that belong to the function. FUNCNEST limits how deep functions
may nest (1000 by default); a function that calls itself for ever fails with
a message instead of crashing the shell.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.hashrc
User configuration file, a shell script read and executed on interactive shell
startup: aliases, environment variable exports, shell options (see
.BR set ),
functions, conditionals and any other command.
.TP
.I ~/.hash_history
Command history file, written as commands are entered, in bash's extended
format: a line with the time before each entry.
.TP
.I /etc/profile, ~/.hash_profile, ~/.profile
Read by a login shell (\-l): /etc/profile first, then ~/.hash_profile, or
~/.profile when there is no ~/.hash_profile.
.TP
.I ~/.hash_logout
Run when a login shell exits.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hash-shell/hashrc.example
Example configuration file with common aliases and settings.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Home directory, used for ~ expansion and default cd destination.
.TP
.B PATH
Colon-separated list of directories to search for commands.
.TP
.B PS1
Primary prompt string.
.TP
.B PS2
Continuation prompt, for a command that spans lines.
.TP
.B PS4
Prefix of the lines printed by
.BR "set \-x" .
.TP
.B HISTFILE
Where the history is saved (default: ~/.hash_history).
.TP
.B FUNCNEST
Maximum function nesting depth, eval and . included (default 1000; 0 keeps
only the stack guard).
.TP
.B HISTSIZE
Maximum number of commands to keep in memory (default: 1000).
.TP
.B HISTFILESIZE
Maximum number of commands to save in history file (default: 2000).
.TP
.B HISTCONTROL
Controls history behavior. Values can be combined with colons:
.RS
.TP
.B ignorespace
Don't save commands starting with a space.
.TP
.B ignoredups
Don't save duplicate consecutive commands.
.TP
.B ignoreboth
Equivalent to ignorespace:ignoredups.
.TP
.B erasedups
Remove earlier duplicate entries from history.
.RE
.TP
.B EDITOR
Default text editor.
.TP
.B LANG
Locale settings.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Running Commands
.nf
# Simple command
$ ls -la

# Pipeline
$ cat file.txt | grep pattern | wc -l

# Command chaining
$ make && make test || echo "Build failed"

# Background job
$ long_command &
.fi
.SS Scripting
.nf
#!/usr/bin/hash-shell

# Process command-line arguments
if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "Usage: $0 <filename>" >&2
    exit 1
fi

# Loop through files
for file in "$@"; do
    if [ -f "$file" ]; then
        echo "Processing: $file"
        wc -l "$file"
    fi
done
.fi
.SS Configuration (~/.hashrc)
.nf
# Aliases
alias ll='ls -lah'
alias gs='git status'
alias ..='cd ..'

# Environment
export EDITOR=vim
export PATH=$HOME/bin:$PATH

# Prompt with git integration
set PS1='\eW\eg \ee#>\ee '

# Shell options
set colors=on
set welcome=off

# Functions and conditionals work too
mkcd() { mkdir -p "$1" && cd "$1"; }
if [ -d "$HOME/bin" ]; then export PATH="$HOME/bin:$PATH"; fi
.fi
.SH STARSHIP PROMPT
.B hash-shell
supports the Starship cross-shell prompt. When Starship is installed
and configured, it automatically integrates with hash-shell for
advanced prompt customization.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hash-shell
returns the exit status of the last command executed, or:
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
General errors.
.TP
.B 2
Misuse of shell command (e.g., syntax error).
.TP
.B 126
Command found but not executable.
.TP
.B 127
Command not found.
.TP
.B 128+n
Command terminated by signal n.
.SH SETTING AS DEFAULT SHELL
To use
.B hash-shell
as your default login shell:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# Add to /etc/shells
sudo bash -c 'echo "/usr/bin/hash-shell" >> /etc/shells'

# Change your shell
chsh -s /usr/bin/hash-shell
.fi
.RE
